The clock that carries the symbols from the CSIPHY to the CSID stayed on the crystal

Between the CSIPHY and the CSID there is a path, and that path has its own
clock: cphy_rx. On the SM7150 nobody ever asked it for a frequency, so it stayed
parked on the crystal, at 19,2 MHz.

Measured live with clk_summary, with the pipeline really streaming:

    camcc_ife_0_clk_src         380000097     <- set
    camcc_ife_0_csid_clk_src    300000000     <- set
    camcc_csi1phytimer_clk_src  300000000     <- set
    camcc_camnoc_axi_clk_src    150000000     <- set
    camcc_cphy_rx_clk_src        19200000     <- the crystal

The cause is in the resource table. csid_set_clock_rates() only calls
clk_set_rate() if the entry has some frequency:

    } else if (clock->nfreqs) {
            clk_set_rate(clock->clk, clock->freq[0]);
    }

and nfreqs is counted stopping at the first zero, so { 0 } literally means "do
not ask it for anything". The SM7150 had { 0 } for vfe0_cphy_rx and
vfe1_cphy_rx. The sdm845 -which is the same block and DOES capture in mainline-
asks for 400000000 for that same clock.

WHY THIS EXPLAINS THE SYMPTOM
-----------------------------
With the link at 845,7 Mbps per lane the byte clock runs at ~106 MHz. At 19,2
MHz the CSID cannot sample anything, and -this is the important part- since the
receive path is never clocked at all, it does not LATCH ERRORS either.

That fits exactly what had been measured and fitted nothing else:

    the sensor emits         frame counter advancing at ~30/s
    the CSIPHY receives      the five lanes leave the rest state when emitting,
                             and their interrupts follow the frame rate
    the CSID sees NOTHING    packets=0, crc=0, MISR=0, and status register
                             0x20 reads 0x00000000 WHOLE

A receiver connected to a transmitter that does emit, even with the wrong rate
or swapped polarity, latches sync errors or bad CRCs. Absolute zero is not "I
receive badly": it is "the signal does not reach me". And that is why sweeping
the settle count, the link rate, the lane count (2, 3 and 4) and the PHY
selector never moved a single bit: all those settings act on a receiver that was
not working.

The SM7150 supports 19,2 / 300 / 384 / 400 MHz for this clock, so the same 400
MHz as the sdm845 is used.

A module parameter to sweep the frequency at runtime is added as well, plus a
trace that records the rate that is REALLY left after asking for it: asking for
a frequency does not guarantee having it, and this clock had been on the crystal
forever without anything saying so.
